From e6bbdb4c91a6d079cdc028700fb1301038e544a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitri <dimitri@moving-in.nl>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 16:02:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 29/54] Shared colonies polish: ground districts, separate
 them, self-heal sockets
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three fixes from watching it run with a real neighbour:

- Floating districts: a visiting colony is anchored far from the ship where
  the terrain rolls, and a slab pinned to y=0 there floats over a dip or is
  swallowed by a rise, with the crew stranded on the ground beside it. Plots
  now sit on the terrain height under their root cell (Plot.groundY), and the
  deck, buildings, crew footing (groundAt), labels and banner all measure up
  from it. Near the ship groundY is ~0, so home is unchanged. Verified: deck,
  buildings and astronauts all land on the same surface.
- Closer: the district anchor ring drops from 7 to 5, so a neighbour's world
  sits nearer your own instead of a long pan away.
- Whose is whose: the repo list splits into your own repos and a labelled
  section per visiting colony (online dot, 'visiting' tag), and a guest row
  drops the 'Colony · ' prefix since the heading carries it.
- Reconnection: after sleep or a network drop the guest listener and the
  discovery socket could die, and the only cure was toggling sharing off and
  on — on both machines. They now self-heal: the guest server restarts a
  dropped listener, discovery rebinds, and a 15s reconcile tick re-asserts
  the intended state. The visitor already retries, so a district returns on
  its own once the sharer's socket is back.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
