From fe8ecb936c2535ebd2198529449b2d1af8ba94ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Kohnstamm <kohnsts@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 08:15:22 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 41/54] Drag a zone to new ground: hold to lift, ghost shows
 where it may land
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zones were placed by the allocator and only the allocator — the layout was
sticky, but never yours. Now a ~250ms hold on a plot (deck or name plate)
lifts it, a translated ghost of its footprint tracks the cursor — green
where the drop is legal, red where it is not — and release lands it. Escape,
or a second finger, puts it back.

A drop must leave the colony one landmass: allocateCells throws the whole
layout memory away when the map fragments, so a split is refused at the
ghost rather than answered with a full re-layout. The same rules refuse the
ship's cell, another zone's cells, and ground past the allocator's pool
(which would silently lose the zone on the next pass). They live in
plot-move.js, pure like merge-state.js, with the lattice facts they rest on
— so they run under bare node, where plots.js's texture pipeline cannot.

The move itself is one plotCells translation plus the standard roster pass:
the signature diff rebuilds the plot on its new ground, navigation and crew
sites recompute so the astronauts walk over, and the layout diff writes the
colony file. Mid-carry everything is cosmetic (a y-offset, dimmed
materials, buildings riding along by position) and a poll landing then is
parked until the drop, so a rebuild can never tear the lifted plot down
under the drag's own hands.

