From 3c71777820ebecdbe5522aa53ec6ca77630d2bff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Kohnstamm <kohnsts@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 08:14:50 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 43/54] A desert world: twin suns, dunes, and adobe buildings
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Karak is the fourth planet, and most of it is the data change the preset
system promised — tan ground, ochre rock, a hazy warm sky. Three things
needed real code:

**Dunes.** The far-field relief grows a `terrain: 'dunes'` variant: ridged
noise (1-|fbm|) sampled in a rotated, squashed frame, so every crest runs
the same way the wind blew it. Getting there forced a refactor that was
overdue anyway — `createTerrain` and `sampleHeight` carried two copies of
the same height recipe with a stay-in-sync comment doing the work of a
function call. The mesh loop now calls `sampleHeight` itself, so the field
anything stands on and the field the mesh was displaced by are one
function. Verified bit-identical for moon, mars and terra against the
pre-refactor code.

**A second sun.** A companion flagged `sunTwin` leaves its parking spot and
rides the sun's own arc, a fixed 13° of azimuth behind it — azimuthal so
its elevation always equals the sun's and the pair rises and sets as one.
It glows with the day rather than against it, and fades across the horizon
on the same ramp as the sun's disc. Ordinary companions keep their fixed
perch and their night glow untouched.

**Adobe.** Two shared uniforms — a tint colour and an amount — pull the
neutral hull swatches (white, grey, slate) toward the planet's clay, by the
same luminance-preserving mix the accent repaint uses and just ahead of it,
so panels keep their shading and trim keeps its colour. Shared like uNight
is shared: switching planet re-themes every standing building with two
writes and no rebuild, and amount 0 makes the other worlds a true no-op.

Scatter gets a sparse `desert` recipe — ochre-tinted rocks, dry grass, the
odd bush, no trees — and the planet picker becomes a 2x2 so the fourth
world does not strand alone on a row.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
